    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <h1>Shrinking quorum</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A redundancy policy for unattended missions with no repair path: vote on three replicas, compare on two,
      self-check on one. It keeps running on the last replica, where fixed TMR stops.
    </p>
    <p>
      Kani proves that, with two or more replicas alive, <code>decide()</code> gives the same outcome under both
      policies, for every set of three replies. The shrinking quorum also sets <code>diagnose</code> at two replicas, so
      it can retire a stuck replica and continue where fixed TMR stays in disagreement. Over a whole run, never less
      than fixed TMR is tested, not proved.
    </p>

    <h2 id="threshold">The trouble with a fixed threshold</h2>
    <p>
      Classic triple modular redundancy (TMR) runs three processors on the same work and takes the majority. Two can
      still detect a disagreement. One cannot outvote anything, so the system stops.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most fault tolerance assumes someone can step in eventually. With a ground team in reach, stopping is safe: the
      team finds the fault and commands a fallback. With no one in reach, stopping throws away the last processor&#39;s
      whole remaining life.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fixed TMR is the baseline on these pages because both policies give the same outcome while two or more replicas
      live. It is not what most spacecraft fly. Most fly dual-string radiation-hardened processors with EDAC, watchdogs
      and autonomous fault protection. Stepping down from three to two to one is not new either: the Space Shuttle&#39;s
      redundant set of general-purpose computers did it, and Meyer gave degradable systems a measure, performability, in
      1980. See <a href="/concepts/prior-art/">Prior art</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      What Mru adds: the step-down runs with no ground team, the decision core is small and checked with Kani, and the
      trade data is published (<a href="/simulation/dusk/">Dusk</a>, <a href="/core/bench/">bench</a>).
    </p>

    <h2 id="modes">Three modes</h2>
    <p>
      The policy picks a mode from the number of live replicas. This is the whole rule, from <code>quorum::mode</code>:
    </p>
    <div class="tbl-box" tabindex="0">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Live replicas</th>
            <th>Fixed TMR</th>
            <th>Shrinking quorum</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">3</td>
            <td>Vote · masks one bad result</td>
            <td>Vote · masks one bad result</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">2</td>
            <td>Compare · loses the tick on a mismatch</td>
            <td>Compare · runs a known-answer test to find the bad one</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="hl">
            <td class="m">1</td>
            <td style="color: var(--ink-faint)">Halted, for good</td>
            <td>Self-check · computes twice, delivers on a match, half speed</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="m">0</td>
            <td>Halted</td>
            <td>Halted</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <pre
      class="pre"
      tabindex="0"
      style="margin-top: 20px"
    ><span class="k">pub fn</span> mode(policy: Policy, alive: usize) -&gt; Mode {'{'}
<span class="k">match</span> (policy, alive) {'{'}
    (_, 0) =&gt; Mode::Halted,
    (Policy::Shrink, 1) =&gt; <span class="s">Mode::SelfCheck</span>,
    (Policy::Tmr, 1) =&gt; Mode::Halted,
    (_, 2) =&gt; Mode::Compare,
    _ =&gt; Mode::Vote,
{'}'}
{'}'}</pre>

    <h2 id="degradation-first">Degradation-first</h2>
    <p>The shrinking quorum applies the whitepaper&#39;s design principle:</p>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="tiny">Principle</span>
      <span>
        &quot;A system intended to operate for millennia without intervention must treat its own physical decay, of
        power, computation, memory, and thermal capacity, not as an exceptional fault to be handled, but as the
        constant, governing condition around which every architectural decision is made.&quot;
      </span>
    </div>
    <div class="callout" style="margin-top: 12px">
      <span class="tiny">Corollary</span>
      <span>
        &quot;Capability is a depleting resource to be spent, not a fixed budget to be defended. The objective is not to
        preserve function but to maximize the cumulative useful work extracted across the entire descent from full
        capability to silence.&quot;
      </span>
    </div>
    <p class="tiny" style="margin-top: 10px">
      W. Binns, <a href="https://mru.space/mru-whitepaper.pdf">Mru whitepaper</a>, section 5,
      doi:10.5281/zenodo.20579438. Dashes in the original are shown as commas.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      For the shrinking quorum, the figure of merit is useful work over the whole decline, not correct operation at full
      strength. <a href="/simulation/dusk/">Dusk</a> measures it, along with the wrong results it costs.
    </p>

    <h2 id="guarantees">What is proved and what is tested</h2>
    <h3 id="proved">Proved (per tick)</h3>
    <p>
      Four Kani proofs on <code>decide()</code>. Each reply is any of <code>Absent</code>, <code>Single</code> or{' '}
      <code>Pair</code>, with any <code>u64</code> values, so each proof covers every set of three replies to{' '}
      <code>decide()</code>.
    </p>
    <ul class="sig-list">
      <li>
        <a href={`${proofs}#L331`}>
          <code>identical_while_tmr_can_run</code>
        </a>
        : with two or more replies present, both policies give the same outcome. It compares the outcome only, not the
        mode or <code>diagnose</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={`${proofs}#L343`}>
          <code>tmr_stops_below_two</code>
        </a>
        : with fewer than two replies present, the fixed TMR outcome is <code>Halted</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={`${proofs}#L352`}>
          <code>shrink_halts_only_with_nothing_left</code>
        </a>
        : the shrinking quorum&#39;s outcome is <code>Halted</code> if and only if no reply is present.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={`${proofs}#L362`}>
          <code>delivers_only_agreed_values</code>
        </a>
        : if either policy delivers a value, at least two replies agree on it, or exactly one replica is alive and it
        returned <code>Pair(v, v)</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      CI runs <code>cargo kani -p quorum -p quorum-ffi</code> on every change. Five more proofs show that the C
      interface gives the core&#39;s answer. The proofs do not cover the health record, the diagnosis, the known-answer
      test, timing, or the system over a run. See <a href="/core/quorum-crate/#proofs">Kani proofs</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="tested">Tested (per run)</h3>
    <p>Never less useful work than fixed TMR over a whole run is a test result, with these scopes:</p>
    <ul class="sig-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Scenario tests.</strong> <code>scripts/scenarios.sh</code> in CI: ten seeds, two kill faults per run,
        20,000 ticks. Kill faults only.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Bench.</strong> 150 pairs of runs per host, five fault schedules, on three hosts with general-purpose
        processors. The three replicas are processes on one host, not separate processors. No pair below fixed TMR. See{' '}
        <a href="/core/bench/#results">Bench</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Dusk.</strong> 10,000 simulated missions. The two policies are identical until fixed TMR stops, by
        construction of the model, and a unit test checks it over 200 seeds. See <a href="/simulation/dusk/">Dusk</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="two-replicas">Two replicas</h2>
    <p>
      With two replicas left, <code>decide()</code> compares them. Equal values are delivered. Unequal values give{' '}
      <code>Detected</code>, and nothing goes out that tick. Under the shrinking quorum it also sets{' '}
      <code>diagnose</code>. Fixed TMR does not.
    </p>
    <p>
      The demo then runs a known-answer test (KAT) on both replicas: a fixed input whose result is known. There are
      three cases.
    </p>
    <ul class="sig-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Both pass.</strong> Nothing is retired. The tick is lost, and nothing escalates. A persistent fault that
        the KAT cannot see keeps every tick <code>Detected</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Both fail or time out.</strong> Both are retired, and the run halts. Fixed TMR would keep comparing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One fails.</strong> It is retired. The survivor goes on in self-check.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px">
      This is the current behavior. The first two cases are gaps: no escalation for a fault the KAT cannot see, and a
      halt where fixed TMR would go on.
    </p>

    <h2 id="cost">What it costs</h2>
    <p>
      On one replica, a stuck fault is caught only by the periodic known-answer test. Until then, a few wrong results
      can get out. Fixed TMR never produces those, because it never lives that long.
    </p>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="tiny">Trade</span>
      <span>
        In Dusk&#39;s default run, fixed TMR comes out ahead only if one wrong result costs more than 136 years of
        useful work (95% CI 131 to 142). The run assumes no ground support after launch. Planned: a per-mission risk
        metric from Dusk, the probability of at least one wrong result.
        <!-- RISK-METRIC -->
      </span>
    </div>

    <h2 id="command-path">Command path</h2>
    <p>
      Self-check on one replica can deliver a few wrong results before the KAT catches a stuck fault. The KAT runs every{' '}
      <code>--kat-period</code> ticks, 64 by default. An upset that hits both self-check runs alike can also get
      through.
    </p>
    <p>
      This mode is for data products, not spacecraft commands, unless the mission adds its own command safety layer.
      Integrators can gate commands on <code>Verdict.mode</code>: issue a command only when the mode is{' '}
      <code>Vote</code> or <code>Compare</code> and the outcome is <code>Deliver</code>. See the{' '}
      <a href="/core/quorum-crate/#gate">example</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="health">Health and diagnosis</h2>
    <p>
      A replica outvoted <code>STRIKES</code> = 3 times within <code>STRIKE_WINDOW</code> = 200 ticks is diagnosed with
      a known-answer test. Pass, and its strikes are cleared, on the assumption that the upsets were transient. Fail,
      and it is retired. The strike record has unit tests, not proofs.
    </p>

    <h2 id="gdi">Graceful Degradation Index</h2>
    <p>
      The whitepaper scores a degrading system by the ratio of capability delivered to hardware surviving. 1.0 means
      every surviving part is doing useful work. Dusk measures the simpler cousin, total useful work across the whole
      decline.
    </p>

    <h2 id="open-questions">Open questions</h2>
    <ul class="sig-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Common-mode faults</strong>, such as shared power, clock or thermal. Dusk models correlated deaths with{' '}
        <code>--p-corr</code>. The demo models an upset that hits both self-check runs alike with{' '}
        <code>--common-mode</code>. Neither models shared power, clock or thermal faults.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Input agreement between replicas.</strong> In the demo, all replicas get the same input because it is a
        function of the tick. Real sensor inputs need an agreement step. Mru does not provide one yet.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Replica determinism under interrupts and scheduling.</strong> The replicas must compute the same bits.
        In the demo this holds only because the task is a pure function of the tick. Neither Dusk nor the bench tests
        it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>KAT coverage on real hardware.</strong> Dusk sets self-check coverage as a parameter,{' '}
        <code>--coverage</code>. The bench&#39;s stuck fault returns the same wrong value for every input, so the KAT
        always sees it. Neither measures the share of real hardware faults a KAT catches.
      </li>
    </ul>

